# Copy to .env.agent next to this file and fill in. Never commit .env.agent.
#
#   cp observability/.env.agent.example observability/.env.agent
#
# On an app host that is where these values belong. **In this repo it is not**:
# `make demo-up` loads agent/.env.agent automatically, so a VPS URL in it turns the
# local demo into a production pusher and leaves `make demo-verify` querying an
# empty local Prometheus. Here, keep those values in agent/.env.agent.production
# and reach them deliberately with `make demo-up REMOTE=1`.
#
# Everything here is read inside the container: config.alloy reads the OBS_*
# variables with sys.env(), and postgres_exporter reads the DATA_SOURCE_* ones.
# Nothing is interpolated by Compose, so a `$` needs no escaping.

# ── Identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The same three values the app's own OBS_APP / OBS_ENV / OBS_HOST must carry —
# keep them in one place if the app is configured from this file too. They are
# stamped onto every metric and every log stream this agent forwards.
OBS_APP=
OBS_ENV=production
OBS_HOST=

# ── Where to push ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Native upstream paths, unrewritten. OBS_TRACES_URL is a base URL: the OTLP
# exporter appends /v1/traces itself.
OBS_PROM_URL=https://ingest.example.com/api/v1/write
OBS_LOKI_URL=https://ingest.example.com/loki/api/v1/push
OBS_TRACES_URL=https://ingest.example.com

# One credential per app+env. scripts/add-ingest-user.sh on the server mints it
# and prints both halves — this one, and the hash to add to the server's
# INGEST_USERS. The username identifies this app in the server's access log, which
# is how push volume gets attributed.
OBS_INGEST_USER=
OBS_INGEST_PASSWORD=

# Leave this alone. It disables certificate verification on all three writers, and
# exists only so the stack's own end-to-end test can push through Traefik on a
# laptop, where Let's Encrypt cannot have issued for the real domain. Turning it on
# against a real endpoint makes basic auth worthless — the credentials above become
# readable by whoever terminates the connection.
#OBS_INGEST_TLS_INSECURE=false

# ── Optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The network the agent shares with the app. Unset matches every network, which is
# right on a host this app has to itself; set it on a host that runs anything else.
#
# Two effects, and the second is the one worth setting it for on a shared box:
#
#   metrics — Docker emits one scrape target per (network × exposed port), so a
#             container on two networks yields duplicate samples for one series
#             and Prometheus rejects the write.
#   logs    — unset, this agent tails EVERY container on the host and stamps this
#             app's identity on all of them. Two apps with two agents means each
#             one ships the other's logs under its own name.
#
# The cost of setting it: a container with no interface on this network is no
# longer collected at all, logs included.
#OBS_DOCKER_NETWORK=

# A single static target for something that cannot be discovered — a process on
# the host, a container that cannot EXPOSE its port. Unset disables it.
#OBS_EXTRA_TARGET=host.docker.internal:9100
#OBS_EXTRA_SERVICE=worker

# ── profile: postgres ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# postgres_exporter's own variable names. The role is created by
# postgres-exporter-init.sql; the password below has to match what that ran with.
DATA_SOURCE_URI=db:5432/appdb?sslmode=disable
DATA_SOURCE_USER=postgres_exporter
DATA_SOURCE_PASS=
